  "problem": "The migration table below describes the movement of the population between cities and suburbs every year in a certain state. \n\n<table align=\"center\" cellpadding=\"10\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td></td>\n<td></td>\n<td colspan=\"2\" align=\"center\">From:</td> \n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td></td>\n<td></td>\n<td width=\"60\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" align=\"center\"><b>Cities</b></td>\n<td width=\"60\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" align=\"center\"><b>Suburbs</b></td> \n</tr>\n<tr> \n<td rowspan=\"2\" align=\"center\">To:</td>\n<td width=\"60\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" align=\"center\"><b>Cities</b></td>\n<td align=\"center\">$0.8$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$0.7$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr> \n<td width=\"60\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" align=\"center\"><b>Suburbs</b></td>\n<td align=\"center\">$0.2$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$0.3$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n\nThat is, $20%$ of those living in cities move to the suburbs each year, and $70%$ of those living in suburbs move to the cities.\n\nAssuming that the population of the state is $9$ million and remains constant, how many people would eventually live in cities if the given probabilities remain constant over many years?",
